    "q-127228": {
      "dom_id": "step-q127228",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(3,0),(3,2),$ and $(0,2),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+12xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14157147/assets/23066b23aebf332921c22a0439a414ade8dffd2a270641d186da0ed0e9488821.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=12xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x{y}^{2}+12x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
